24
00:00:52,794 --> 00:00:54,868
It genuinely helps the channel.

25
00:00:55,518 --> 00:00:59,451
Jensen Huang was born in 1963 in Tainan,

26
00:00:59,451 --> 00:01:00,365
Taiwan.

27
00:01:01,015 --> 00:01:03,429
As a child, he and his older brother were

28
00:01:03,429 --> 00:01:05,842
sent to the United States to live with an

29
00:01:05,842 --> 00:01:06,336
uncle.

30
00:01:06,986 --> 00:01:09,272
The uncle enrolled them at a school in

31
00:01:09,272 --> 00:01:11,448
rural Kentucky that he thought was a

32
00:01:11,448 --> 00:01:13,176
prestigious boarding school.

33
00:01:13,826 --> 00:01:16,064
It turned out to be a religious academy

34
00:01:16,064 --> 00:01:17,236
for troubled youth.

35
00:01:17,886 --> 00:01:20,282
Later, as a teenager in Oregon, Huang

36
00:01:20,282 --> 00:01:22,559
worked at Denny's — washing dishes,

37
00:01:22,559 --> 00:01:25,194
bussing tables, and waiting on customers.

38
00:01:25,844 --> 00:01:28,416
He has often said those jobs taught him

39
00:01:28,416 --> 00:01:30,008
humility and hard work.

40
00:01:30,658 --> 00:01:33,173
He studied electrical engineering, worked

41
00:01:33,173 --> 00:01:34,430
as a chip designer,

42
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:37,580
and by his late twenties he had an idea

43
00:01:37,580 --> 00:01:39,664
about where computing was going.

44
00:01:40,314 --> 00:01:43,325
In 1993, Huang and two engineers, Chris

45
00:01:43,325 --> 00:01:46,407
Malachowsky and Curtis Priem, met at a

46
00:01:46,407 --> 00:01:49,558
Denny's in San Jose and decided to start a

47
00:01:49,558 --> 00:01:50,329
company.

48
00:01:50,979 --> 00:01:52,386
They called it NVIDIA.

49
00:01:53,036 --> 00:01:55,727
Their bet: video games would need special

50
00:01:55,727 --> 00:01:57,562
chips to draw 3D graphics —

51
00:01:58,212 --> 00:02:00,688
chips that could do thousands of simple

52
00:02:00,688 --> 00:02:02,634
calculations at the same time.

53
00:02:03,284 --> 00:02:04,940
The early years were brutal.

54
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:07,130
Their first product struggled, and the

55
00:02:07,130 --> 00:02:09,214
company came close to running out of

56
00:02:09,214 --> 00:02:09,695
money.

57
00:02:10,345 --> 00:02:12,685
For years afterward, Huang reportedly

58
00:02:12,685 --> 00:02:15,083
opened staff meetings with a line that

59
00:02:15,083 --> 00:02:16,546
became company legend:

60
00:02:17,196 --> 00:02:19,336
"Our company is thirty days from going

61
00:02:19,336 --> 00:02:20,406
out of business."

62
00:02:21,056 --> 00:02:24,702
In 1999, NVIDIA launched the GeForce 256

63
00:02:24,702 --> 00:02:28,348
and marketed it as the world's first GPU

64
00:02:28,348 --> 00:02:30,941
— a graphics processing unit.

65
00:02:31,591 --> 00:02:34,284
Gamers loved it. But the most important

66
00:02:34,284 --> 00:02:36,913
thing about the GPU wasn't graphics at

67
00:02:36,913 --> 00:02:37,362
all.

68
00:02:38,012 --> 00:02:41,184
A normal processor, a CPU, is like a few

69
00:02:41,184 --> 00:02:44,285
brilliant workers doing complex tasks one

70
00:02:44,285 --> 00:02:45,484
after another.

71
00:02:46,134 --> 00:02:49,263
A GPU is like thousands of simple workers

72
00:02:49,263 --> 00:02:51,610
doing small tasks all at once.

73
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:54,883
That turned out to be exactly what another

74
00:02:54,883 --> 00:02:57,273
field needed: the kind of math used to

75
00:02:57,273 --> 00:02:58,730
train neural networks.

76
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:02,638
In 2006, NVIDIA released CUDA, software

77
00:03:02,638 --> 00:03:05,821
that let scientists use GPUs for general

78
00:03:05,821 --> 00:03:08,190
computing, not just graphics.

79
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:11,539
It cost a lot to build, and for years many

80
00:03:11,539 --> 00:03:13,578
investors didn't see the point.

81
00:03:14,228 --> 00:03:16,706
Then, in 2012, a small team at the

82
00:03:16,706 --> 00:03:19,718
University of Toronto — Alex Krizhevsky,

83
00:03:19,718 --> 00:03:22,597
Ilya Sutskever and Geoffrey Hinton —

84
00:03:23,247 --> 00:03:26,069
trained a neural network called AlexNet

85
00:03:26,069 --> 00:03:28,570
on two NVIDIA gaming graphics cards.

86
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:31,724
It crushed the competition in a major

87
00:03:31,724 --> 00:03:33,539
image recognition contest.

88
00:03:34,189 --> 00:03:36,513
Two gaming cards had helped kick off the

89
00:03:36,513 --> 00:03:38,027
deep learning revolution.

90
00:03:38,677 --> 00:03:40,672
And NVIDIA was already holding the

91
00:03:40,672 --> 00:03:41,265
shovels.

92
00:03:41,915 --> 00:03:44,267
There's an old saying about gold rushes:

93
00:03:44,267 --> 00:03:46,729
the people who got rich weren't always the

94
00:03:46,729 --> 00:03:48,808
miners — they were the ones selling

95
00:03:48,808 --> 00:03:49,409
shovels.

96
00:03:50,059 --> 00:03:54,180
When ChatGPT launched in late 2022, the

97
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:56,241
AI gold rush began.

98
00:03:56,891 --> 00:03:59,800
Every major tech company suddenly needed

99
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:02,506
enormous numbers of advanced chips to

100
00:04:02,506 --> 00:04:04,332
train and run AI models.

101
00:04:04,982 --> 00:04:06,678
And NVIDIA made the best ones.

102
00:04:07,328 --> 00:04:09,762
Data centers filled with its chips began

103
00:04:09,762 --> 00:04:11,573
appearing all over the world.

104
00:04:12,223 --> 00:04:15,289
In 2023, NVIDIA's value passed one

105
00:04:15,289 --> 00:04:16,947
trillion dollars.

106
00:04:17,597 --> 00:04:21,118
On October 29, 2025, it became the first

107
00:04:21,118 --> 00:04:24,722
company in history to pass five trillion.

108
00:04:25,372 --> 00:04:27,721
Huang, in his signature black leather

109
00:04:27,721 --> 00:04:29,659
jacket, became one of the most

110
00:04:29,659 --> 00:04:32,302
recognizable executives on Earth — and one

111
00:04:32,302 --> 00:04:33,359
of the richest.

112
00:04:34,009 --> 00:04:36,462
So how does one chip company shape the

113
00:04:36,462 --> 00:04:38,376
world? In at least four ways.

114
00:04:39,026 --> 00:04:41,882
First, AI itself. A huge share of the

115
00:04:41,882 --> 00:04:44,669
world's most advanced AI is trained on

116
00:04:44,669 --> 00:04:45,961
NVIDIA hardware.

117
00:04:46,611 --> 00:04:49,413
When NVIDIA can't make chips fast enough,

118
00:04:49,413 --> 00:04:51,769
AI progress slows down everywhere.

119
00:04:52,419 --> 00:04:55,129
Second, money. NVIDIA became one of the

120
00:04:55,129 --> 00:04:57,654
largest companies in the stock market.

121
00:04:58,304 --> 00:05:00,603
Millions of people's retirement savings

122
00:05:00,603 --> 00:05:02,903
move up and down with its share price —

123
00:05:02,903 --> 00:05:04,710
often without them knowing it.

124
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:08,387
Third, geopolitics. Since 2022, the

125
00:05:08,387 --> 00:05:11,565
United States has restricted exports of

126
00:05:11,565 --> 00:05:14,516
the most advanced AI chips to China,

127
00:05:15,166 --> 00:05:17,607
and NVIDIA has had to design special,

128
00:05:17,607 --> 00:05:19,744
weaker versions for that market.

129
00:05:20,394 --> 00:05:22,441
At the same time, its chips are

130
00:05:22,441 --> 00:05:24,429
manufactured mostly in Taiwan.

131
00:05:25,079 --> 00:05:27,617
A single company sits right on the fault

132
00:05:27,617 --> 00:05:29,920
line between the world's two largest

133
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:30,687
economies.

134
00:05:31,337 --> 00:05:34,076
Fourth, energy. The data centers filled

135
00:05:34,076 --> 00:05:36,504
with these chips use vast amounts of

136
00:05:36,504 --> 00:05:37,437
electricity.

137
00:05:38,087 --> 00:05:40,877
The International Energy Agency has

138
00:05:40,877 --> 00:05:43,521
projected that global data center

139
00:05:43,521 --> 00:05:46,605
electricity use could roughly double by

140
00:05:46,605 --> 00:05:48,807
2030, driven largely by AI.

141
00:05:49,457 --> 00:05:51,596
Chips are not just products anymore.

142
00:05:51,596 --> 00:05:53,954
They are power — in every sense of the

143
00:05:53,954 --> 00:05:54,393
word.

144
00:05:55,043 --> 00:05:57,370
When a company becomes this important,

145
00:05:57,370 --> 00:05:59,583
something interesting happens in our

146
00:05:59,583 --> 00:06:02,137
heads: we start to see it as one person.

147
00:06:02,787 --> 00:06:05,566
In 1840, the Scottish writer Thomas

148
00:06:05,566 --> 00:06:08,701
Carlyle argued that "the history of the

149
00:06:08,701 --> 00:06:11,908
world is but the biography of great men."

150
00:06:12,558 --> 00:06:14,520
This became known as the Great Man

151
00:06:14,520 --> 00:06:15,655
theory of history.

152
00:06:16,305 --> 00:06:18,538
Critics, like the philosopher Herbert

153
00:06:18,538 --> 00:06:19,899
Spencer, pushed back:

154
00:06:19,899 --> 00:06:21,914
great people are products of their

155
00:06:21,914 --> 00:06:22,622
societies.

156
00:06:23,272 --> 00:06:25,744
Without the right conditions, no single

157
00:06:25,744 --> 00:06:27,334
person changes anything.

158
00:06:27,984 --> 00:06:30,404
Psychology adds another twist.

159
00:06:30,404 --> 00:06:33,557
In 1920, psychologist Edward Thorndike

160
00:06:33,557 --> 00:06:35,830
described the halo effect:

161
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:38,822
when we see one impressive quality in a

162
00:06:38,822 --> 00:06:40,830
person, we tend to assume they're

163
00:06:40,830 --> 00:06:42,670
impressive in other ways too —

164
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:45,304
smarter, more trustworthy, more moral.

165
00:06:45,954 --> 00:06:48,453
A brilliant engineer and visionary CEO

166
00:06:48,453 --> 00:06:51,195
starts to look like someone who can't make

167
00:06:51,195 --> 00:06:51,926
mistakes.

168
00:06:52,576 --> 00:06:55,178
And investors, employees and governments

169
00:06:55,178 --> 00:06:57,841
begin to rely on that one person far more

170
00:06:57,841 --> 00:06:58,809
than is safe.

171
00:06:59,459 --> 00:07:03,172
In early November 2026, that halo cracked.

172
00:07:03,822 --> 00:07:06,523
On November 8, Huang sent a note to NVIDIA

173
00:07:06,523 --> 00:07:08,624
staff admitting that he had been

174
00:07:08,624 --> 00:07:10,185
unfaithful to his wife.

175
00:07:10,835 --> 00:07:12,767
On its own, that would have been a private

176
00:07:12,767 --> 00:07:13,196
matter.

177
00:07:13,846 --> 00:07:16,144
But several U.S. outlets reported

178
00:07:16,144 --> 00:07:18,059
something far more serious:

179
00:07:18,709 --> 00:07:21,283
that the woman involved was suspected by

180
00:07:21,283 --> 00:07:23,617
American officials of having ties to

181
00:07:23,617 --> 00:07:25,114
Chinese intelligence —

182
00:07:25,764 --> 00:07:27,958
and that a company linked to her had

183
00:07:27,958 --> 00:07:30,152
bought billions of dollars of NVIDIA

184
00:07:30,152 --> 00:07:30,658
chips,

185
00:07:31,308 --> 00:07:33,864
amid long-running concerns about advanced

186
00:07:33,864 --> 00:07:35,898
chips being smuggled into China.

187
00:07:36,548 --> 00:07:38,929
She has not been charged, and the company

188
00:07:38,929 --> 00:07:41,040
linked to her has denied wrongdoing.

189
00:07:41,690 --> 00:07:44,400
NVIDIA's board launched an independent

190
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:46,978
investigation, led by a committee of

191
00:07:46,978 --> 00:07:49,424
independent directors with outside

192
00:07:49,424 --> 00:07:50,152
lawyers.

193
00:07:50,802 --> 00:07:53,248
The company said it had no reason to

194
00:07:53,248 --> 00:07:56,072
believe that national security or NVIDIA's

195
00:07:56,072 --> 00:07:58,079
secrets had been compromised.

196
00:07:58,729 --> 00:08:00,594
Still, the reaction was fast.

197
00:08:00,594 --> 00:08:02,867
Lawmakers called on Huang to testify

198
00:08:02,867 --> 00:08:03,975
before Congress.

199
00:08:04,625 --> 00:08:06,428
A shareholder lawsuit was filed.

200
00:08:07,078 --> 00:08:09,513
And on November 11, The Wall Street

201
00:08:09,513 --> 00:08:11,948
Journal reported that the board was

202
00:08:11,948 --> 00:08:14,508
preparing to remove him as CEO, citing

203
00:08:14,508 --> 00:08:16,694
people familiar with the matter.

204
00:08:17,344 --> 00:08:19,675
Further reports described a separate

205
00:08:19,675 --> 00:08:22,066
relationship with an NVIDIA employee.

206
00:08:22,716 --> 00:08:24,921
NVIDIA's stock, which had already been

207
00:08:24,921 --> 00:08:26,266
sliding, fell sharply.

208
00:08:26,916 --> 00:08:29,835
In after-hours trading on November 11, it

209
00:08:29,835 --> 00:08:32,622
stood roughly a third below its October

210
00:08:32,622 --> 00:08:33,152
high.

211
00:08:33,802 --> 00:08:36,155
As of this recording, no final decision

212
00:08:36,155 --> 00:08:37,387
has been announced.

213
00:08:38,037 --> 00:08:40,432
Engineers have a name for a part of a

214
00:08:40,432 --> 00:08:42,528
system that, if it fails, brings

215
00:08:42,528 --> 00:08:44,804
everything down: a single point of

216
00:08:44,804 --> 00:08:45,523
failure.

217
00:08:46,173 --> 00:08:48,366
Good systems are designed to avoid them.

218
00:08:49,016 --> 00:08:51,086
Investors have a similar idea:

219
00:08:51,086 --> 00:08:53,910
key person risk — when a company's value

220
00:08:53,910 --> 00:08:56,232
depends heavily on one individual.

221
00:08:56,882 --> 00:08:59,370
For years, NVIDIA's story was told as

222
00:08:59,370 --> 00:09:00,863
Jensen Huang's story.

223
00:09:00,863 --> 00:09:03,103
His vision, his bets, his jacket.

224
00:09:03,753 --> 00:09:06,066
That story helped build one of the most

225
00:09:06,066 --> 00:09:07,885
valuable companies in history.

226
00:09:08,535 --> 00:09:10,931
But it also meant that a crisis in one

227
00:09:10,931 --> 00:09:13,444
person's life could shake markets, worry

228
00:09:13,444 --> 00:09:14,321
governments,

229
00:09:14,971 --> 00:09:17,908
and raise questions about the security of

230
00:09:17,908 --> 00:09:20,846
the technology the whole AI industry runs

231
00:09:20,846 --> 00:09:21,246
on.

232
00:09:21,896 --> 00:09:24,182
Whatever the board decides, the lesson is

233
00:09:24,182 --> 00:09:25,377
bigger than one man.

234
00:09:26,027 --> 00:09:28,350
When a society builds its future on a

235
00:09:28,350 --> 00:09:30,965
single company — and a single company on a

236
00:09:30,965 --> 00:09:32,010
single person —

237
00:09:32,660 --> 00:09:34,875
it isn't only betting on their genius.

238
00:09:34,875 --> 00:09:37,035
It's betting on their every decision.

239
00:09:37,685 --> 00:09:39,020
Let's try something.

240
00:09:39,020 --> 00:09:41,573
Think about something you depend on every

241
00:09:41,573 --> 00:09:43,779
day — your job, a team, a family, a

242
00:09:43,779 --> 00:09:44,417
project.

243
00:09:45,067 --> 00:09:47,282
Now ask: is there one person, or one

244
00:09:47,282 --> 00:09:49,668
thing, that if it suddenly disappeared,

245
00:09:49,668 --> 00:09:51,087
everything would stop?

246
00:09:51,737 --> 00:09:53,883
If the answer is yes, you've found your

247
00:09:53,883 --> 00:09:55,263
single point of failure.

248
00:09:55,913 --> 00:09:58,125
And the fix is usually the same for

249
00:09:58,125 --> 00:09:59,756
companies and for people:

250
00:09:59,756 --> 00:10:01,736
share knowledge, build backups,

251
00:10:02,386 --> 00:10:04,714
and don't confuse one person's brilliance

252
00:10:04,714 --> 00:10:06,196
with a system's strength.

253
00:10:06,846 --> 00:10:09,420
A dishwasher from Denny's helped build the

254
00:10:09,420 --> 00:10:11,650
machine at the center of the AI age.

255
00:10:12,300 --> 00:10:14,176
That part of the story is real, and

256
00:10:14,176 --> 00:10:14,868
remarkable.

257
00:10:15,518 --> 00:10:17,877
But the most important chapter may be the

258
00:10:17,877 --> 00:10:19,593
one being written right now —

259
00:10:20,243 --> 00:10:22,581
not about how one man rose, but about

260
00:10:22,581 --> 00:10:25,154
whether the systems around him are strong

261
00:10:25,154 --> 00:10:27,084
enough to survive without him.

262
00:10:27,734 --> 00:10:30,146
Because in the end, the most powerful

263
00:10:30,146 --> 00:10:32,558
technology in the world still runs on

264
00:10:32,558 --> 00:10:34,909
something very old and very fragile:

265
00:10:35,559 --> 00:10:36,602
human beings.

